 When a training distribution has to cover several stressors, its designer chooses whether the policy meets them together or one at a time, and the choice matters most when they interact. Ecology studies the interactive and cumulative effects of multiple stressors <d-cite key="crain2008stressors"></d-cite><d-cite key="cote2016stressors"></d-cite>, and climate science studies compound events and their risk in their own right <d-cite key="zscheischler2018compound"></d-cite><d-cite key="zscheischler2020typology"></d-cite>. In reinforcement learning the choice is a question of zero-shot generalization <d-cite key="kirk2023zsg"></d-cite>: must a policy see stressors co-occur in training to cope with their co-occurrence at test time? Gao et al. (2024) <d-cite key="gao2024composition"></d-cite> studied it for imitation-learned manipulation; we study it for survival in multi-agent reinforcement learning.
 
-Where the hazard sits in the learning problem decides what such a comparison can teach. In some fire-related work the hazard is the task, as for aerial agents rewarded for moving to burning trees on a fire's boundary <d-cite key="haksar2018"></d-cite>; safe reinforcement learning typically places it in a cost or a constraint <d-cite key="garcia2015safe"></d-cite>. In the Compound Hostile Environment (CHE) it does neither. The fire is an *ambient hazard* (Definition 2, §3): a fixed stochastic process with nothing in it optimizing against the agents, carried by no cost or constraint channel and invisible to the reward, which reads task variables and agent deaths and prices every death at $d_p = 0.5$<!-- N:ENV-1 -->, whatever its cause. Such a hazard is not new in itself; what it gives this study is a comparison in which no reward or cost signal mentions the hazard in either arm. Proposition 1 shows that the fire, which looks like nonstationarity to any one agent, is state, so CHE is a finite Dec-POMDP; with communication denied it does not reduce to a multi-agent POMDP, and finite-horizon Dec-POMDPs are NEXP-complete <d-cite key="bernstein2002"></d-cite>, so we learn policies rather than plan (Remark 1).
