-**A hazard that feeds on itself.** Coupling A lets structural collapse add ignitions. The nearest neighbour we found, Raut et al. (2026) <d-cite key="raut2026psps"></d-cite>, model public-safety power shutoffs as a single-agent decision problem in which the power flows that follow the operator's switching decisions set the failure probability of lines in wildfire areas, which the authors treat as ignition risk; their reward is negative operating cost, and there is no spatial spread model. CHE's agents influence the hazard too, but weakly and indirectly: an occupied weak cell collapses nine times<!-- N:CA-8 --> as often per step as an empty one (Definition 5), yet agent load adds only 0.4–1.4 %<!-- N:CA-9 --> to the collapses per episode under the trained policies of §5, and 7.4–8.3 %<!-- N:CA-9 --> under random policies. Since a collapse may seed fire, we do not claim that CHE's hazard grows without agent influence. The difference from Raut et al. is one of directness and size: there the controlled decision sets the failure probability, while in CHE ignition is not an action available to the agents, and where they stand moves the collapse count only slightly.
